{"annotationDigest":"sha256:bf2094eab06eb3cbb0fcf1e74796e848cb438f31013d3db6119bfd48cceecc41","annotationId":"rann-adc08e6562e9c7a509db66f43cf93efa","author":{"agent":"claude-code","kind":"ai","model":"claude-opus-5.5"},"createdAt":"2026-10-02T00:05:51Z","disclaimer":"AI-generated review opinion derived from the evidence package. It is not a verdict, does not change readiness, and must be confirmed by a human reviewer.","disclosureCheck":{"blocking":1,"contradictions":[],"disclosures":5,"nextDecision":"more_evidence","readiness":"not_assessed"},"findings":[{"claim":{"params":{"candidate":"unmapped_code|test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","path":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","position":"spec_gap"},"type":"unmapped_code"},"detail":"Disclosure `unmapped_code` (prevents_ready) and candidate `unmapped_code|test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh`. The script asserts the behaviour the PR adds: a symlinked root is offered, selected and format-filtered; symlinked and real roots merge; an interior symlink loop keeps exit 0; a broken symlink argument is rejected with 'Path not found'; overlapping and literally duplicated roots each give one row. SW-REQ-260922-HR29 ('matches the requested formats case-insensitively, prunes dotfiles and dot-directories, and sorts rows by modification time descending; rejects a nonexistent path on stderr with exit 1') states none of these. Position spec_gap: the behaviour is intended (PR body: 'Overlapping roots (e.g. Pictures → Videos) → single row per file'; issue #12665), so the code is not wrong and the script is not noise. The fix is to amend HR29, or add a child requirement under SYS-REQ-260922-6642 that says command-line symlink roots are followed, interior symlinks are not descended, and each file is listed once per device:inode across roots. Then add a `# Verifies:` tag to the script so its rows join the requirement. A human records the decision.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":1},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"high","title":"The PR's new test — the only execution evidence for its purpose — traces to no requirement","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"unmapped_code|test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"candidate"},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file"}],"method":"review.scope (changed units, intent graph, spec-gap candidates): specGapCandidates","note":"The package flags unmapped_code|test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh (unmapped_code, severity medium): New file test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh is traced to no requirement: behaviour without intent. The author's position (spec_gap) is judgement, not verified; a human decision settles it.","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"`bin/omarchy-menu-file` carries `Implements: SW-REQ-260922-HR29`, a file-level trace (weak_mapping). HR29 is `observed_pass` (2 passed) on `test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh`, which runs real directories only: no symlink root, no aliasing, no hard links. Its obligation cells error_handling (inherited from SYS-REQ-260922-6642) and external_call_timeout_bounded are `not_linked`, so neither is tested. The trace re-review (0a1a0cae) restamped HR29 and is our proof work, not upstream.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"test"}],"severity":"medium","title":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 is the requirement the changed script implements; its passing evidence does not exercise the change","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 is in review.changes (code_changed, matchLevel file).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"`test/node/menumodel-replay.test.mjs` is traced at file level (weak_mapping) to 20 MenuModel/Menu requirements. a33e56a7 (our proof work, not the upstream change) appends a test() there that spawns the PR's script. That puts these requirements in scope as code_changed: SW-REQ-260922-3T3F, SW-REQ-260922-46HY, SW-REQ-260922-74BZ, SW-REQ-260922-7NPE, SW-REQ-260922-CYB9, SW-REQ-260922-DQ9P, SW-REQ-260922-E4J2, SW-REQ-260922-EFNR, SW-REQ-260922-JRW1, SW-REQ-260922-N3RM, SW-REQ-260922-PRNV, SW-REQ-260922-SJ7P, SW-REQ-260922-TKDP, SW-REQ-260922-XW52, SW-REQ-260922-Z680, SYS-REQ-260922-0M8A, SYS-REQ-260922-PPDW, SYS-REQ-260922-R8DQ, SYS-REQ-260922-V7W6, SYS-REQ-260927-WC89. No MenuModel.js or Menu.qml code changed. All of them are `observed_pass`; cells marked `not_linked` there were not linked at base either. That test()'s result joins to these 20 requirements, not to HR29.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-3T3F","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-46HY","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-74BZ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-7NPE","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-CYB9","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-DQ9P","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-E4J2","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-EFNR","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-JRW1","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-N3RM","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-PRNV","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-SJ7P","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-TKDP","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-XW52","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-Z680","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260922-0M8A","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260922-PPDW","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260922-R8DQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260922-V7W6","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/node/menumodel-replay.test.mjs","kind":"file","line":1338}],"severity":"info","title":"20 menu-model requirements are in scope only because our mirror commit edits the replay test file","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SYS-REQ-260927-WC89 is in review.changes (code_changed, matchLevel file).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$root\" \"jpg\" where $root/a/x.jpg and $root/b/x.jpg are hard links to one inode: base offers both paths, head offers one"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"The dedup key `awk -F'\\t' '!seen[$1]++'` on `%D:%i` is device and inode, not root alias. Two distinct paths that are hard links to one inode (e.g. a photo hard-linked into two album folders under one root) were two rows at base (`cut -f2-` printed every path). At head they become one row, and the other path is no longer selectable. The PR states the intent only for aliased roots. HR29 does not decide either way. Only a run settles this.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: do hard-linked files now collapse to one picker row?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Hard-linked files under one root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file","bin/omarchy-transcode"],"scenario":"omarchy-transcode with ~/Videos a symlink to a tree containing a mode-000 subdirectory: the user picks a file, and omarchy-menu-file exits 1 under pipefail so omarchy-transcode aborts"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Under `set -euo pipefail`, the pipeline status is the rightmost non-zero status. `find -H ... 2>/dev/null` exits 1 on 'Permission denied' while still printing the readable rows. sort, awk and cut succeed, and `omarchy-menu-select` returns the pick. The script would then exit 1, and `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` (`input=$(omarchy-menu-file ...)` under `set -e`) would abort after the user picked a file. This already happens for real-directory roots. -H makes it reachable through symlinked roots (e.g. `~/Videos -> /mnt/disk` with a root-owned `lost+found`), which at base listed nothing. The PR's interior-loop row covers loops, not permissions.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: can a pick succeed and the script still exit 1 when a followed symlink root contains an unreadable directory?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"two files with identical mtime under one root, and aliased roots Pictures->Videos: compare row order and the surviving path spelling at base and head"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Base `sort -rn` reversed the last-resort whole-line comparison, so equal-mtime rows ordered by path descending. Head `sort -t$'\\t' -k2,2rn` makes `r` key-local, so ties fall back to an ascending whole-line comparison that starts with `D:i`. Equal-mtime files now order by device:inode string. For two aliases of one file, the lexically smaller path line is kept first and the other is dropped. HR29 states mtime-descending only, so this is not a requirement break. It does decide which path (`Pictures/...` or `Videos/...`) the picker returns. GNU sort (coreutils gsort) confirms the sort semantics on synthetic rows (`9:1 Pictures/b` kept over `9:1 Videos/b`; base `sort -rn` orders `/r/b` before `/r/a`); the full find pipeline was not run here (no GNU find on this host).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: which alias spelling survives the dedup, and how do equal-mtime rows order now?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$tmp/link.jpg\" \"jpg\" where link.jpg is a symlink to a real jpg: base offers no row, head offers one"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"`-H` dereferences every command-line path, not only directories. A root that is a symlink to a regular file with a matching extension was `-type l` at base (not listed) and is `-type f` at head (listed as one row). The `[[ -e $path ]]` check accepts it either way. The PR describes directory roots only.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":32}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed now?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Symlink-to-file root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"review.compat is `compatible`: test_diff, mcdc_diff, removed_behavior and differential were all checked, with 0 findings and 213 inputs over 5 partitions. The configured behaviour-diff harness (`test/bdiff/harness.mjs`) covers only `shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js` and `Menu.qml`, so none of those inputs ran the changed script. `review.behaviorDiff.inventory` is empty. The removed expressions (plain `find` = -P, `-printf '%T@\\t%p\\n'`, `sort -rn`, `cut -f2-`) were never run base-vs-head by the engine. The hazards above are what such a run would have to settle. Context, not an approval: only a human reviewer approves compatibility.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":47},{"id":"test/bdiff/harness.mjs","kind":"file"}],"severity":"info","title":"Compatibility 'compatible' carries no differential evidence for bin/omarchy-menu-file","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"`coverage_unmeasured`: `review.diffCoverage.unmeasuredFiles` = [bin/omarchy-menu-file]. The 0 uncovered lines/outcomes figure therefore says nothing about lines 47-56. `effects_unavailable`: `review.effects` is absent (call-level effects are C/C++ only), so no new-call, gained-effect or caller-chain facts exist for this bash change. The scope approximations note that uncodified connections cannot be detected without them. Callers found by reading: `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` and `bin/omarchy-games-retro-install:22` (`|| exit 0`).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-games-retro-install","kind":"file","line":22}],"severity":"info","title":"Changed-code coverage of the script was not measured, and effects were not analyzed","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The test() added in a33e56a7 requires exit 0, at least one `^ok - ` line and no `^not ok` line. The script's two self-skips (`no GNU find` and `find lacks -printf`) print `ok - ...` through base-test.sh `pass`, so the wrapper also passes when nothing ran. On this run the evidence points to a real run: testResults record 54 passed and 0 skipped, and `menu-file-test.sh`, whose suite step is gated on the same GNU find -printf support, ran and passed. The orchestrator reports 8/8 ok on the Linux box, but the per-row output is not in the package. Requiring `! /# SKIP|skipping/` (or the exact 8 row names) would close this.","refs":[{"id":"test/node/menumodel-replay.test.mjs","kind":"file","line":1342},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":8},{"id":"test/node/menumodel-replay.test.mjs","kind":"test"}],"severity":"info","title":"Our wrapper test passes on a self-skip too (opinion on our proof work)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The body still says 'Dedup rows after `cut -f2-` with `awk '!seen[$0]++'`'. That path-string dedup was what 09f6913 shipped, and its own 'overlapping roots dedupe rows' row failed (count=2) in our earlier review. Head 03e6af6 keys on `%D:%i` before `cut -f3-`, and the row passes. Worth updating so the merged history describes the shipped mechanism.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":52}],"severity":"info","title":"PR description no longer matches the head (opinion)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"`-printf` was already GNU-only, and the PR adds `%D`/`%i`, which are also GNU (the menu-file suite step is gated on GNU find). For context: BSD find silently skips loop directories under -L with exit 0, which is why a -L draft would pass on macOS/BSD hosts. The `checks_skipped` disclosure (14 gates, e.g. documentation_coverage, security_surface_covered) is configuration and unchanged from the baseline. Not caused by this PR.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":7},{"id":"documentation_coverage","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"Pre-existing: GNU find dependency; 14 checks skipped by configuration","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}}],"packageDigest":"sha256:9d03d7e234598b8aa5d93abffc0b923008205e45f88fabbcf3c81dc4c5f28d19","packageIdentity":"epkg-489332d26ace4d431e7d20779d7c6c8d8781fc8a4195a2a6f55cc8f6c09e327c","riskAssessment":{"level":"medium","rationale":"A small, well-motivated pipeline change with its own 8-row test. The test traces to no requirement (unmapped_code, prevents_ready); coverage of the script was not measured, effects were not analyzed, and the differential harness does not cover the script. Untested edges: hard-link collapse, permission-denied exit under pipefail via followed roots, tie order and alias spelling, symlink-to-file roots."},"schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-annotation.v1","suggestedTests":[{"forClaim":3,"rationale":"Settles whether %D:%i dedup drops a distinct, selectable path; decide and record the intended behaviour in the requirement.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Hard-linked files under one root"},{"forClaim":4,"rationale":"mkdir a mode-000 dir under the symlink target, pick a file with a stub selector, and assert omarchy-menu-file's exit status (and omarchy-transcode's).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169}],"title":"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root"},{"forClaim":5,"rationale":"touch -d the same time on two files and on an aliased pair, then assert row order and which path the stub selector receives, at base and head.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling"},{"forClaim":6,"rationale":"Pass a symlink to a matching file as the path argument; assert whether it is offered.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54}],"title":"Symlink-to-file root"}],"summary":"**What the PR does.** Upstream #13197 (Bartok9, part of #12665) makes `bin/omarchy-menu-file` list files under symlinked roots (e.g. `~/Videos -> /mnt/media`). Before, GNU find (default -P) skipped a symlinked start point, so `omarchy transcode` got an empty picker. It switches to `find -H`: start points are followed, interior symlinks are not descended. It also prints `%D:%i` and keeps one row per device:inode under the mtime-descending sort, so aliased roots (`Pictures -> Videos`) give one row per file. It adds `test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh` (8 rows). Our proof work on the mirror (a33e56a7, 0a1a0cae) runs that script unmodified from a test() in `test/node/menumodel-replay.test.mjs` and re-reviews the HR29 trace. The PR description is stale: it still describes a path-string `awk '!seen[$0]++'` dedup.\n\n**Is the fix verified?** Partly. Readiness is `not_assessed`, and the next decision is `more_evidence` (missing_execution_evidence) because of `unmapped_code`. The new script is the only test of the symlink-root and dedup behaviour, and it traces to no requirement. SW-REQ-260922-HR29's `observed_pass` rests on `menu-file-test.sh`, which has no symlink row. Its error_handling and external_call_timeout_bounded cells are `not_linked`. Coverage of `bin/omarchy-menu-file` was not measured (`coverage_unmeasured`), and change effects were not analyzed (`effects_unavailable`). The package records compatibility `compatible` (0 findings), but its differential harness only runs MenuModel.js and Menu.qml, so it says nothing about this script. The removed-behaviour inventory is empty.\n\n**Questions for a human.** Do hard-linked files now collapse to one row? Does a permission-denied subdirectory under a followed symlink root make the picker exit 1 under pipefail after a pick? Which spelling survives for aliased roots, and how do equal-mtime rows order now? Is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed?\n\n**Traceability.** No requirement says that symlinked roots are followed or that rows are deduped by inode. HR29 covers formats, dotfile pruning, mtime order and nonexistent-path rejection only. Position: spec_gap. Amend HR29 or add a child requirement, then tag the script `Verifies:`.\n\n**Not caused by this PR.** 14 checks are skipped by configuration. HR29's obligation cells were `not_linked` at base. The GNU find dependency (`-printf`, now also `%D`/`%i`) is pre-existing."}
